I think the PR #196  I did fixed things like DKL to at least run, but was a bad fix because we'll fail to get priors for parameters in gpytorch.Modules that are children of torch.nn.Modules. For example, consider AdditiveKernel, which places a bunch of Kernels in a ModuleList. Since named_parameter_priors is a recursive call that won't actually run on the ModuleList because it doesn't exist, we won't actually get priors for the component Kernels even if they are defined.
Ordinarily I'd just implement a fix, but I'm actually not sure how to deal with this one. Even if we implement an in house ModuleList extension, it seems dangerous and insidious to leave this failure case in, since the intention is that GPyTorch Modules are interoperable with torch ones. Therefore, a recursive version of named_parameter_priors seems not possible -- maybe we have to expand each child of a normal torch Module in the method directly?
@Balandat

See the discussion in #174. When calling backward to get the derivative of the mean function with respect to a test input, we enter the backward pass of InvMatmul. There doesn't seem to be an obvious reason we should be doing this, since none of the linear solves involved in the predictive mean depend on the test point.
This suggests we might also he computing derivatives with respect to model hyperparameters, which would be wasted computation.
@Balandat

Persuant to the discussion in #113, Exact GPs should compute the various covariance matrices needed for the predictive distribution separately. This could save a lot of time and space for applications like BayesOpt, and also make the white noise kernel implementation a little easier since we wouldn't have to pad the diagonal with t zeros.
@gpleiss and I talked, and I think a great solution is to only create matrices as they are needed. Basically exact_gp.py would create some object like a LazyKernelEvaluationVariable, whose __getitem__ and diag methods would explicitly call the kernel.  E.g., my_lazy_evaled_kernel[:n_train, :n_train] would actually call the kernel on k(x_train, x_train).
In addition to space and time savings, with this kind of construct, something like the code as written by @Balandat in #113 for the white noise kernel would work.

There should be a simple API for combining kernels, which allows concise syntax and a lot of flexibility, while at the same time preventing users from shooting themselves in the foot (e.g. break closure by subtracting kernels, etc).
@darbour has some prototype of this, which defines the appropriate operators on the base Kernel class, e.g.:
# in Kernel
    def __add__(self, other):
        return AdditiveKernel(self, other)

class AdditiveKernel(Kernel):
    def __init__(self, kernel_1, kernel_2):
        super(AdditiveKernel, self).__init__()
        self.kernel_1 = kernel_1
        self.kernel_2 = kernel_2

    def forward(self, x1, x2):
        return self.kernel_1(x1, x2) + self.kernel_2(x1, x2)
